the eDraft Help Center. Two stages — a read-only review report first, then a lock for the rewrite. Do not edit anything in stage 1.
What exists: the help content is one structured module, `src/lib/help/articles.ts` (categories, articles, body blocks as plain data — this same data will later generate the Apple Help Book, so it must stay serializable; renderers in `src/routes/help/` and `src/lib/help/*.svelte` never invent content). Treat that architecture as fixed and good.
The standard you are writing to: Apple's own help. Study how Apple structures help for its pro apps (support.apple.com/guide/final-cut-pro, /guide/pages — task-based titles as gerunds or imperatives, one task per article, numbered steps, second person, short sentences, zero marketing voice, a "Tip" only when it genuinely saves the reader). eDraft is an Apple-platform app and its help must feel like it ships from Apple, per docs/MACOS-DESIGN.md's voice.
Stage 1 — review report, covering:

1. Accuracy audit (the priority): check every factual claim in every article against the actual code — menu paths, shortcuts, button names, behaviors, with file:line evidence. Flag every claim that's wrong, stale, or unverifiable. Recent behavior you must reflect: IL-0095's collapsed-cut editing rules, the IL-0094 two-page sheets (no flag anymore), omit/restore via Navigator hover / context menu / Format menu, the page-lock notice (IL-0090).
2. Coverage gaps: compare the category map against the app's actual feature surface (menus in MainMenu.swift, the Format/Element menu, launch window actions). Notably missing today: page locks / Freeze Page state (the IL-0090 notice will need a "Learn More" article — slug it now), act breaks, the two-page/grid/continuous views in depth, Revert To / versions.
3. Competitive scan (web research): how Final Draft's knowledge base, Arc Studio, Highland 2, Slugline, Fade In, WriterDuet, and Scrivener structure their help and what topics they consider essential for screenwriters that we're missing (e.g. production workflows, revision colors, watermarks, collaboration handoff). Also tryarqo.com. One paragraph per competitor: structure, voice, what to steal, what to avoid. Never copy their text — insights only.
4. IA + style verdict: are the 8 categories and popular-articles list the right ones? Are titles task-based? Where does the voice slip into marketing?

Deliver the report as your reply with a concrete proposed article map (slug → title → category → one-line scope), including what to rewrite, what to add, what to delete. Then STOP and present a Vibe Check for stage 2 (the rewrite/expansion lock, budget covering articles.ts and only touching renderers if a new block type is truly needed). Wait for my yes before stage 2.
House rules: Code Director lock for any file change, no commit without my explicit word, don't touch other sessions' files (apple/** is another session's active area — read it for evidence only).